WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.703
Hi, I'm Dan Wahlin from GitHub.

00:00:02.703 --> 00:00:08.193
In the next few minutes, I'll show
you how the GitHub Copilot app lets

00:00:08.193 --> 00:00:12.507
you put AI agents to work, for
developers, project managers, business

00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:13.683
analysts, and leaders.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.141
Let's start with a question.

00:00:17.141 --> 00:00:23.023
How much of your day is real work,
and how much goes to hunting for

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:26.553
information, switching between
tools, and waiting on other people?

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.903
Every role has work that waits.

00:00:29.903 --> 00:00:32.256
Developers have bugs and missing tests.

00:00:32.256 --> 00:00:35.001
Project managers build
status reports by hand.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.746
Analysts move data between
spreadsheets and systems.

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:40.491
And leaders wait on all of it.

00:00:40.491 --> 00:00:45.589
A chat assistant helps with answers,
but the work still lands on you.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:47.766
Here's the idea.

00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:50.512
You bring the ideas and the judgment.

00:00:50.512 --> 00:00:52.080
Agents do the steps.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.825
Multiply the two, and you get impact.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.791
Derek Sivers said it well:

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.536
ideas are just a multiplier of execution.

00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:05.242
Agents raise the execution side for
everyone, not only for people who

00:01:05.242 --> 00:01:06.026
write code.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.344
The GitHub Copilot app is one
desktop workspace for many agents.

00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:16.834
You hand off a task, run several at
once, and review the results before

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.010
you accept them.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.363
Think of yourself as a conductor.

00:01:21.363 --> 00:01:23.716
A conductor doesn't play every instrument.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.142
You set the direction, and
each section plays its part.

00:01:28.142 --> 00:01:32.847
In today's demo, one agent builds
a website, another builds an Excel

00:01:32.847 --> 00:01:36.377
workbook, another builds an
executive briefing, and another reads

00:01:36.377 --> 00:01:37.945
Microsoft 365 with WorkIQ.

00:01:37.945 --> 00:01:40.691
An automation keeps the beat every week.

00:01:40.691 --> 00:01:43.044
And you stand in the middle:

00:01:43.044 --> 00:01:45.004
you plan, review, and approve.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.353
Our story uses one public dataset:

00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:53.451
the FHFA House Price Index and CFPB
mortgage data, for all fifty states

00:01:53.451 --> 00:01:55.412
and one hundred metro areas.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.763
Here's how you work with agents.

00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:01.116
You describe the outcome you want.

00:02:01.116 --> 00:02:04.253
For bigger work, you read the plan first.

00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:10.135
Then you hand it off to Autopilot,
and the agents work at the same time.

00:02:10.135 --> 00:02:14.841
When they finish, you review the
diffs, previews, and files, and then

00:02:14.841 --> 00:02:16.802
you merge, share, or deploy.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.153
Let's go to the live demo.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.722
I start with the session
that gets the public data.

00:02:24.722 --> 00:02:29.035
I pick the demo-start branch, choose
Autopilot, and paste the prompt.

00:02:29.035 --> 00:02:34.918
I also start a second session that
uses WorkIQ, and I'll come back to it

00:02:34.918 --> 00:02:35.310
later.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.908
The agent reads the instructions in
the repo, writes a small Node.js

00:02:40.908 --> 00:02:45.614
script with no extra packages, and
downloads the data straight from FHFA

00:02:45.614 --> 00:02:46.790
and the CFPB.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.928
There's no API key, and no manual download.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.567
In about two minutes, the data is ready.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:58.528
The agent checked every file:

00:02:58.528 --> 00:03:02.842
the row counts, the first and
last period, and missing values.

00:03:02.842 --> 00:03:07.548
It wrote a data dictionary, then
pushed everything to a branch called

00:03:07.548 --> 00:03:11.077
live-data, so the other
agents can start from it.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.567
Now I start three more
sessions from live-data:

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:22.273
the website in Plan mode, and the
Excel workbook and the executive

00:03:22.273 --> 00:03:23.450
briefing in Autopilot.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.940
Each one works on its own branch,
in its own worktree, so they never

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:29.332
collide.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.752
For the website, the agent
shows me its plan first:

00:03:36.752 --> 00:03:39.889
the tasks, the files, and the deploy steps.

00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:44.987
It looks good, so I approve it, and
the agent switches to Autopilot.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:53.588
Now four agents work at the same
time, each in its own session.

00:03:53.588 --> 00:03:54.765
Watch the sidebar:

00:03:54.765 --> 00:03:59.470
the website agent writes code and
tests, the Excel agent builds a

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:03.784
workbook, the briefing agent builds
slides, and the WorkIQ agent reads

00:04:03.784 --> 00:04:05.353
my Microsoft 365 data.

00:04:05.353 --> 00:04:10.451
While they work, I'm free to do
something else, like talk to you.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.169
The website is done.

00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:19.659
The agent ran its tests and the build,
deployed the site to Azure Static

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:24.757
Web Apps, and then opened the live
site in the app's browser panel.

00:04:24.757 --> 00:04:29.855
It even checked its own work in
the browser and fixed two layout

00:04:29.855 --> 00:04:31.424
problems before it deployed.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.096
Here's the map.

00:04:36.096 --> 00:04:40.802
When I press play, it animates home
price changes for every quarter

00:04:40.802 --> 00:04:42.763
since the year two thousand.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.750
I can open any state, like Texas, to
see its price trend compared with

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:56.848
the U.S., and its mortgage loans
by purpose from 2018 to 2025.

00:04:56.848 --> 00:05:02.338
Below the map are the fifteen metro
areas with the largest change in the

00:05:02.338 --> 00:05:03.123
last year.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.973
The Excel workbook is done too.

00:05:08.973 --> 00:05:14.463
The agent even opened it in Excel
to make sure that it opens without

00:05:14.463 --> 00:05:14.855
errors.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.536
Here's the dashboard.

00:05:16.536 --> 00:05:22.419
When I pick a state, like Texas,
Florida, or New York, the KPI cards and

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:23.203
charts update.

00:05:23.203 --> 00:05:27.909
There's a real PivotTable and
PivotChart, and a heat map with a

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:29.478
sparkline for every state.

00:05:29.478 --> 00:05:32.223
That's work that takes an analyst hours.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.289
Now the WorkIQ session.

00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:42.387
It searched my email, Teams, and
calendar for this talk, and wrote a

00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:43.171
one-page brief.

00:05:43.171 --> 00:05:48.661
My prompt told it to use roles only,
never names, and to skip anything

00:05:48.661 --> 00:05:49.053
confidential.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:53.726
Here's the brief.

00:05:53.726 --> 00:05:58.825
It has the goals, the five audience
roles, the date, time, and place,

00:05:58.825 --> 00:06:01.570
and the open questions from the organizers.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:06.275
It lists its sources by type
and date only, with no names.

00:06:06.275 --> 00:06:12.158
Then it compared all of that with my
talk outline, and found nine gaps I

00:06:12.158 --> 00:06:13.726
still need to close.

00:06:13.726 --> 00:06:16.472
That information was spread
across eleven emails.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.823
Last, the executive briefing for leaders.

00:06:19.823 --> 00:06:24.529
Seven slides, each with one headline
that states the insight, and one

00:06:24.529 --> 00:06:24.921
chart.

00:06:24.921 --> 00:06:29.627
These are native PowerPoint charts
that you can edit, and every slide

00:06:29.627 --> 00:06:30.803
has speaker notes.

00:06:30.803 --> 00:06:35.509
And the numbers match the website
exactly, because every agent read the

00:06:35.509 --> 00:06:37.470
same instructions in the repo.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.146
Finally, an automation.

00:06:42.146 --> 00:06:47.245
I give it a name, pick a weekly
trigger, and describe the job:

00:06:47.245 --> 00:06:51.950
every Monday, get new data, rebuild
the website, the workbook, and the

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.911
briefing, and summarize what changed.

00:06:53.911 --> 00:06:57.048
Set it up once, and it keeps running.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.864
So, four results, built at the
same time from one dataset:

00:07:04.864 --> 00:07:09.570
a live website, an analyst workbook,
an executive briefing, and a brief

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.746
from Microsoft 365.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.711
And you stay in control.

00:07:13.711 --> 00:07:16.456
You read the plan before work starts.

00:07:16.456 --> 00:07:19.201
Each agent works on its own branch.

00:07:19.201 --> 00:07:22.338
You choose which tools
can run without asking.

00:07:22.338 --> 00:07:25.475
And nothing is accepted until you review it.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.970
Alan Kay said, the best way to
predict the future is to invent it.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.323
With agents, more people can invent.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:40.026
So pick one task this week
and hand it to an agent.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:44.732
The free course, GitHub Copilot app
for beginners, shows you how, step

00:07:44.732 --> 00:07:45.516
by step.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.834
Bring an idea, and put
an agent to work on it.

00:07:50.834 --> 00:07:52.010
Thanks for watching.
